unit(9,1,'Scope an Azure access and governance plan',
'''Azure RBAC assigns a role to a security principal at a scope. Management group, subscription, resource group, and resource scopes form an inheritance hierarchy. Start with the task and smallest useful scope rather than assigning Owner to make an error disappear. Contributor can manage resources but cannot generally assign Azure RBAC roles. Reader can inspect resources without changing them. A management role does not automatically grant every data-plane permission; reading a storage account configuration differs from reading its blobs.

Azure Policy evaluates resource configuration against organizational rules. A policy can audit or deny configurations, but it does not give a user permission to create resources. Resource locks protect against certain management changes and are not a replacement for authorization or backups. An administrator diagnosing a denied operation should examine the identity, effective role assignment, scope, policy result, and locks. Record which control caused the decision before changing access. Use fictional identities and a paper scope diagram before experimenting in an isolated subscription.''',
'RBAC controls who can act; Policy evaluates allowed resource configurations.~Management-plane access and data-plane access can require different roles.',
'Identify task~Choose principal and scope~Inspect roles and policy~Test allowed and denied actions',
'Draw a subscription with dev and production resource groups.~Assign a fictional developer resource-management access only to dev; identify who may assign roles.~Add a policy restricting locations and predict a denied deployment despite valid RBAC.~Record two positive and two negative authorization tests.',
'A Contributor can create a VM in one location but receives a policy denial in another.~RBAC permits the action; a separate location rule constrains the resource.~Use an allowed location or obtain an approved policy exception, rather than granting Owner.',
'A dev assignment does not unintentionally cover production.~The location denial is distinguished from a missing role.',[
q('An Azure developer should manage resources only in a dev resource group. Which assignment fits?','Contributor at the dev resource group','Owner at the subscription','Reader at the tenant','Contributor at every management group','Contributor at the narrow scope supports resource management without broad assignment privileges.'),
q('Which Azure control can deny deployment in an unapproved location?','Azure Policy','A Reader role','A DNS record','A resource tag alone','Policy can evaluate configuration such as location and deny noncompliant requests.'),
q('Why might Azure Reader access to a storage account not allow downloading a blob?','Management access and blob data access are distinct','Reader always downloads all data','The account must be public','Tags override identity permissions','A data-plane role or another authorized access method may be required.'),
q('A Contributor receives a location-policy denial. What is the targeted response?','Select an allowed location or seek an approved exception','Assign Owner to bypass every policy','Remove all subscription controls','Create a new user with the same role','A broader role does not remove a policy denial.'),
q('A role was assigned at subscription scope, but only dev access was intended. What change reduces excess privilege?','Move the assignment to the dev resource group after checking dependencies','Add an identical assignment at tenant scope','Rename the user','Add a cost tag','Scope limits the set of resources affected by a role assignment.'),
q('A delete action fails although the operator has the required role. A resource has a delete lock. What should be investigated?','The lock and its approved removal process','The browser language','Whether Reader would grant more permissions','The billing currency','Locks and RBAC are separate management controls; identify the actual cause before changing either.')])

unit(9,2,'Separate Azure durability, access, and recovery',
'''An Azure storage design has several independent decisions: the data service, redundancy, access control, network reachability, and recovery policy. Locally redundant storage maintains copies within a location, while zone-redundant storage spreads copies across availability zones in the primary region. Availability and service support vary, so verify the selected region and account capabilities before deployment. Replication is not the same as a backup: an authorized deletion or bad overwrite may be replicated too.

Blob versioning and soft delete can help recover from supported deletion or overwrite scenarios when configured in advance. Retention choices determine the recovery window and cost. A private endpoint gives a service a private network interface, but clients still need working DNS and authorization. Disabling public access without a tested private path can break legitimate clients. A useful acceptance test reads a permitted object, rejects an unauthorized identity, resolves the expected endpoint, and restores a deliberately modified test object. Keep recovery evidence and remove temporary objects after the exercise.''',
'Replication helps durability and availability; recovery settings address supported deletion scenarios.~A private network path does not replace authentication and authorization.',
'Define failure~Choose redundancy~Configure access~Test recovery',
'Create a paper storage design for zone failure and accidental overwrite.~Choose a redundancy approach and a separate retention/versioning policy.~Trace a client through DNS, private endpoint, and data authorization.~Write a restore test with original content, changed content, and an expected recovered version.',
'An application reaches a private endpoint but cannot read a blob.~Network reachability does not establish data authorization.~Inspect the application identity and blob permissions before broadening network exposure.',
'The design names separate availability and deletion-recovery controls.~The test verifies recovered contents, not just a successful command.',[
q('Which Azure redundancy choice spreads copies across availability zones in the primary region?','ZRS','A blob access tier','A resource lock alone','A DNS alias','Zone-redundant storage distributes copies across supported zones.'),
q('Why is replication alone insufficient for an accidental overwrite recovery plan?','An unwanted change may also be replicated','Replication disables all access control','Replication is only a naming service','Replicas never receive writes','Versioning or a suitable recovery design addresses historic versions separately.'),
q('A private endpoint resolves correctly and is reachable, but a blob read returns an authorization error. What should be checked next?','The client identity and data permissions','Whether the endpoint is public','The color of the resource icon','The VM display resolution','A working path does not supply permission to read data.'),
q('When should a storage deletion-recovery feature be configured?','Before the incident, with a suitable retention period','Only after every copy is deleted','After disabling all logs','Only during billing export','Recovery controls generally cannot retroactively recover data outside their protection.'),
q('A team plans to disable public storage networking. Which validation should precede the change?','Test client DNS, private routing, and authorized data access','Only rename the account','Remove identity roles to simplify testing','Assume private DNS works automatically in every network','Private endpoint success depends on the actual client path and DNS design.'),
q('A restore operation reports success. What provides stronger evidence of application recovery?','Read the restored test object and compare expected content','Check only the storage account name','Delete the recovery log','Assume redundancy guarantees the desired version','Successful operation status alone does not prove the restored content is the required version.')])

unit(9,3,'Trace an Azure application failure',
'''Troubleshooting a cloud application starts with the failing conversation, not a random restart. Identify source, destination, protocol, port, and name resolution. Azure network security groups evaluate rules using priority; lower numeric values have higher priority. Check effective rules at relevant interfaces and subnets as well as routes and service configuration. An open rule does not create a listener, and a healthy VM power state does not prove an application is serving requests.

Azure Monitor metrics summarize numeric measurements over time. Logs provide records that can be queried and correlated, when collection has been configured. The Activity Log helps investigate management-plane events such as a configuration change; it is not a complete record of every application request. An alert needs a condition, evaluation settings, and a useful response destination such as an action group. Compare an observed failure to a known baseline and validate both the symptom and telemetry after a targeted correction. Avoid opening all inbound ports as a diagnostic shortcut.''',
'A network allow rule does not start an application listener.~Activity Log management events differ from application request logs.',
'Resolve name~Trace routes and rules~Check listener~Correlate metrics and logs~Verify repair',
'Draw a client, subnet, VM, and HTTPS service with port 443.~Write conflicting NSG rules with priorities 100 and 200 and predict the matching decision.~Build a failure timeline with one management change and one application symptom.~Define an alert condition, recipient, and a test proving the alert works.',
'A VM is running, but HTTPS fails after a network-rule update.~Power state does not establish end-to-end service availability.~Compare effective rules, the route, and the listener; verify an actual HTTPS request after repair.',
'Lower numerical NSG priority is evaluated before a higher number.~The alert test checks notification delivery as well as the metric.',[
q('Two matching Azure NSG rules have priorities 100 and 200. Which is considered first?','100','200','The rule with the longest name','Both are always ignored','Lower priority numbers are evaluated first.'),
q('Where would you first look for an Azure resource configuration change event?','Activity Log','Only a browser bookmark','The VM wallpaper','A blob access tier','Activity Log contains management-plane operations.'),
q('What does a running VM state establish about HTTPS availability?','Only part of the dependency chain is healthy','The certificate must be valid','Every client can reach port 443','The app has passed a transaction test','Power state does not verify rules, routing, listener, certificate, or application behavior.'),
q('A metric alert fires but nobody receives a notification. What is a focused next check?','The action group and notification delivery','The VM hostname length','The storage redundancy setting','Whether every port is open','An alert condition and its response actions are distinct parts of the design.'),
q('HTTPS fails after an NSG change while the VM remains running. Which evidence best narrows the cause?','Effective rules, routing, and a listener check for the failing path','Only CPU model information','A successful ping from an unrelated network','The subscription display name','Follow the actual source-destination conversation and its dependencies.'),
q('The Activity Log shows no configuration changes during an application error. What follows?','Application logs and other telemetry are still needed','The application could not have failed','All user requests succeeded','No further evidence can exist','Management activity is not a complete record of application behavior.')])

unit(10,1,'Design AWS temporary access and trust',
'''AWS IAM policies describe allowed or denied actions on resources, sometimes with conditions. The effective decision depends on applicable policies and evaluation rules; an explicit deny overrides an allow. A role has a trust policy describing who may assume it and permission policies describing what the resulting session may do. These are different questions. A workload should normally use a suitable role and temporary credentials rather than a long-lived access key embedded in application code.

For cross-account access, examine both sides of the relationship and the applicable resource policy or role-assumption design. An allow statement in one place does not guarantee end-to-end authorization. Encryption adds another dependency: reading an object encrypted with a customer managed KMS key may require permission to use that key as well as permission to read the object. Test with the actual workload identity and record denied actions. Do not resolve errors by adding wildcard administrator access; narrow the missing permission or incorrect trust relationship and retest the intended operation.''',
'Role trust determines who can assume a role; permissions determine what its session can do.~Data access and KMS key access can be separate dependencies.',
'Identify workload~Establish trust~Grant narrow actions~Obtain temporary session~Test effective access',
'Draw a fictional workload role and a single permitted S3 bucket.~Write a task-to-permission table and identify who can assume the role.~Add an encrypted object and document the additional key-access dependency.~Predict an allowed read, denied write, and explicitly denied read.',
'A role can read ordinary objects but cannot read a KMS-encrypted object.~Object permission alone may not authorize use of the encryption key.~Inspect KMS permissions and key policy alongside the S3 permission.',
'The design separates role trust from session permissions.~No long-lived key is placed in source code.',[
q('What does an IAM role trust policy principally define?','Who may assume the role','The monthly service price','Every network route','The object retention period','Trust controls role assumption; permission policies govern actions by the assumed session.'),
q('Which credential approach best fits an EC2 application accessing AWS services?','An appropriate instance role with temporary credentials','A root access key in the source repository','A shared permanent admin key in a script','A password stored in a public bucket','Workload roles avoid distributing long-lived access keys.'),
q('An applicable explicit deny conflicts with an allow. What is the authorization result?','Denied','Always allowed','Chosen randomly','Determined by policy name length','Explicit deny overrides an applicable allow.'),
q('S3 object permission is present but a customer-managed KMS encrypted read fails. Which dependency matters?','Permission to use the KMS key','The EC2 instance name tag alone','The browser cache','A larger subnet','Encrypted access can involve both storage and key authorization.'),
q('A role has the required S3 policy, but an external account cannot assume it. Which part needs inspection?','The role trust and caller assumption permissions','Only the S3 storage class','The billing contact name','The application logo','Session permissions cannot help until the caller can legitimately assume the role.'),
q('A developer fixes an AccessDenied error by attaching administrator access. What is the better lasting correction?','Identify the denied action and grant only the necessary scope','Embed the new credentials permanently','Disable encryption on every bucket','Remove all logging','Use the failure evidence to correct narrowly scoped authorization and verify the intended task.')])

unit(10,2,'Design for failures across availability zones',
'''A resilient application must tolerate failures in each dependency, not just duplicate its web servers. An Application Load Balancer can distribute requests to healthy targets across configured availability zones. An Auto Scaling group can replace unhealthy capacity, but the application should avoid keeping essential session state only on a replaceable instance. Externalize state in a suitable shared service and make request handling resilient to retries.

Database availability and read scaling are different needs. A conventional RDS Multi-AZ DB instance deployment uses a standby for failover rather than as a read replica. Other RDS deployment types have different capabilities, so identify the actual product and deployment model. A queue can decouple producers and consumers, allowing work to wait during a temporary processing disruption. Consumers should handle possible redelivery and use idempotent processing where required. Define recovery time and acceptable data loss, then test a component failure. A diagram with two icons is insufficient evidence that the application still works when one dependency disappears.''',
'High availability and read scaling solve different problems.~Queue redelivery requires safe consumer behavior, not an assumption of exactly-once business effects.',
'Define failure target~Distribute capacity~Externalize state~Decouple work~Test failover',
'Draw two availability zones, a load balancer, replaceable app instances, and a database.~Identify all session state and remove single-instance dependencies on paper.~Add a queue and define how duplicate message processing will be detected.~Walk through losing one zone and record expected user-visible behavior.',
'Web instances span two zones, but carts exist only in local process memory.~Replacing a failed instance loses its users’ carts.~Move required session state to an appropriate shared service and test recovery.',
'The design survives instance replacement without losing required session state.~The duplicate-message case has a deliberate handling strategy.',[
q('What should be avoided on replaceable app instances when sessions must survive failures?','Keeping essential session state only in local memory','Using a load balancer','Health checks','Separating compute and storage','Local session state disappears with the instance unless deliberately preserved elsewhere.'),
q('What is the standby in a conventional RDS Multi-AZ DB instance deployment primarily for?','Failover availability','Serving all read-only queries as a read replica','Hosting DNS','Replacing IAM','The standby provides high availability; read replicas are a distinct scaling mechanism.'),
q('Which component can buffer work when processing is temporarily unavailable?','A queue','An instance name tag','A route table description','A password policy','A queue decouples arrival from processing capacity.'),
q('Why should a queue consumer often be idempotent?','A message may be delivered again','Queues always delete databases','Every message arrives only once under all failures','It replaces encryption','Safe repeated processing prevents duplicate business side effects.'),
q('A multi-zone web tier loses user carts during instance replacement. Which dependency was overlooked?','Session state stored on individual instances','The count of region names','The DNS record comment','The cost allocation tag','Compute redundancy alone does not preserve local state.'),
q('A failover test restores service in twelve minutes, but the target is five. What should the team conclude?','The tested design misses the recovery-time target','Two zones guarantee the target regardless of test results','The target refers only to backup size','The test should be discarded','Measured recovery must be compared with the required recovery objective.')])

unit(10,3,'Match AWS storage to access and recovery needs',
'''Storage selection starts with how an application accesses data. S3 provides object storage, EBS provides block volumes for supported compute use cases, and EFS provides a shared file system. An application expecting file-system operations cannot always be moved to an object API without changes. Durability, access latency, sharing, capacity, and recovery requirements all matter. A low storage price alone does not establish the lowest total cost.

Lifecycle transitions can move eligible objects to different storage classes, but retrieval patterns, minimum storage duration charges, and restore latency must be considered. Some archive classes require a restore before data can be read. Backups and versioning address recovery scenarios distinct from ordinary replication. Document the required recovery point and recovery time, then verify a sample restore. Transfer, requests, and retrieval charges may dominate a rarely accessed dataset that is unexpectedly read in bulk. Use a workload estimate and current pricing rather than assuming every archival class is interchangeable. A paper design is sufficient for this introductory exercise; no paid resources are required.''',
'Object, block, and file storage expose different interfaces.~Low stored-byte cost does not imply low retrieval cost or immediate access.',
'Identify interface~Estimate access pattern~Choose storage~Model total cost~Test restore',
'Classify an image archive, a VM disk, and a shared Linux directory by access interface.~Write retrieval frequency and maximum acceptable recovery time for each.~Compare immediate access with delayed archive restoration for the image archive.~Estimate storage, requests, retrieval, and transfer categories without creating resources.',
'An archive must support immediate incident retrieval, but the proposed class requires a restore delay.~The storage price is lower but the access requirement is not met.~Select an appropriate access class or revise the recovery requirement explicitly.',
'The shared directory is not treated as an interchangeable object API.~The cost comparison includes retrieval behavior.',[
q('Which AWS service supplies an object storage interface?','S3','EBS','EFS','An IAM role','S3 stores and retrieves objects rather than exposing a block disk or shared file system.'),
q('An application requires a shared file system for Linux clients. Which service is the closest fit?','EFS','An S3 bucket without application changes','An IAM group','A route table','EFS provides a managed shared file system.'),
q('Which cost category can matter when archived data is unexpectedly read in bulk?','Retrieval and transfer charges','Only the account display name','Only the IAM policy length','Only the dashboard color','Total cost includes access and transfer, not just storage capacity.'),
q('A recovery plan requires immediate access. What must be checked before choosing an archive class?','Whether retrieval requires a restore and its latency','Only the class name','Only the number of object tags','Only the region abbreviation','Archive access behavior must satisfy the recovery-time requirement.'),
q('A program requires block-device semantics for its instance disk. Why is S3 not a drop-in replacement?','Object API operations differ from block-device operations','S3 has no durability','S3 can only store text','EBS is an identity service','Storage interfaces affect how applications read, write, and manage data.'),
q('A backup exists, but nobody has tested reading restored data. What remains unproven?','Whether the restore meets content and recovery-time requirements','Whether the backup has a name','Whether a cost tag can be added','Whether a region has a console','Backup existence is weaker evidence than a validated restore.')])

unit(11,1,'Distinguish identity, verification, and authorization',
'''An identity identifies a person, workload, or device. Authentication establishes confidence in that identity; authorization determines what it may access. Microsoft Entra ID is a cloud identity service. Multifactor authentication combines suitable factors and reduces risks from a stolen password, but it does not replace appropriate access permissions or protect every possible session compromise. Single sign-on reduces repeated sign-ins across supported applications without removing the need for access decisions.

Conditional Access evaluates signals and applies access controls according to policy. A policy might require stronger authentication for a sensitive application or restrict access based on device conditions. These decisions require deliberate scoping, licensing awareness, and testing. Zero Trust principles include verifying explicitly, using least privilege, and assuming breach; a corporate network location alone is not sufficient trust. For a beginner exercise, create a fictional access matrix and test normal, elevated-risk, and emergency-access scenarios on paper. The objective is to explain the decision path, not to configure a live tenant or risk locking out an administrator.''',
'Authentication establishes identity; authorization grants access.~Single sign-on reduces repeated prompts but does not grant every application permission.',
'Identify actor~Authenticate~Evaluate access policy~Authorize resource~Monitor session',
'List fictional staff, contractor, and workload identities.~Build an application access matrix with the minimum required privileges.~Write normal and risky sign-in scenarios and choose verification requirements.~Document how policy testing avoids locking out emergency access.',
'A contractor signs in successfully but cannot open a finance application.~Authentication succeeded; application authorization may still be absent.~Verify the intended assignment rather than treating all successful sign-ins as universal access.',
'The matrix separates sign-in success from resource permission.~Risky and ordinary cases produce justified access decisions.',[
q('A successful sign-in establishes which part of access?','Authentication','Permission to every file','Universal administrator rights','Data classification','Authentication identifies the actor; authorization is a separate decision.'),
q('Which Microsoft service is principally associated with cloud identity?','Microsoft Entra ID','A storage access tier','A virtual network route','An archive mailbox alone','Entra ID manages cloud identities and access capabilities.'),
q('What is a goal of least privilege?','Grant only the access needed for the task','Give every employee administrator access','Avoid all verification','Trust every corporate IP indefinitely','Narrow permissions reduce unnecessary access and impact.'),
q('What does single sign-on normally reduce?','Repeated sign-in prompts across supported applications','Every authorization decision','All endpoint risk','All need for monitoring','SSO simplifies authentication experience without granting universal access.'),
q('A contractor can sign in but cannot use the finance app. What should be checked?','The intended application authorization and assignment','Whether successful authentication must bypass policy','Only the browser theme','The office printer queue','An authenticated identity still requires the appropriate resource access.'),
q('A policy change may affect every administrator. What is an appropriate rollout approach?','Test scope and emergency-access behavior before broad enforcement','Apply broadly with no recovery plan','Remove all monitoring first','Share one permanent password','Access controls require validation so a misconfiguration does not prevent legitimate recovery.')])

unit(11,2,'Choose threat protection by the evidence needed',
'''Security products address different evidence and response needs. Microsoft Defender capabilities cover areas such as endpoints, email, identities, and cloud workloads. Extended detection and response correlates signals across supported security domains. Microsoft Sentinel is a cloud-native SIEM and SOAR platform that can collect and analyze data from multiple sources and support response workflows. The name of a product alone does not mean that every source is connected or every response is enabled.

An alert is a signal to investigate, while an incident can group related activity for analysis and response. A good analyst asks what telemetry supports the hypothesis, how timestamps and entities relate, and what evidence is missing. Automation should have appropriate permissions and escalation boundaries. Blocking an account can contain harm but also interrupt legitimate work, so response actions need context and authority. In this introductory exercise, use invented events and classify them by source. Write a short investigation that separates confirmed actions from uncertain conclusions and identifies the next useful evidence.''',
'SIEM centralizes and analyzes logs; endpoint protection acts on endpoint threats.~An alert is evidence to investigate, not automatic proof of compromise.',
'Collect signals~Correlate entities~Investigate incident~Authorize response~Verify result',
'Create fictional email, endpoint, and identity alerts for one user.~Normalize times and identify which signals refer to the same device or session.~Assign evidence sources to an investigation and write one benign alternative.~Define a response action with an owner, authority, and verification step.',
'A suspicious email and an endpoint alert reference the same user within minutes.~Correlation strengthens an investigation but still needs entity and time validation.~Inspect linked evidence before declaring the scope or automating disruptive containment.',
'The investigation distinguishes a hypothesis from confirmed actions.~The response names an authorized decision-maker.',[
q('Which Microsoft platform is associated with SIEM and SOAR?','Microsoft Sentinel','Azure DNS alone','A file compression utility','A resource tag','Sentinel supports security analytics and orchestration across connected data.'),
q('What does XDR aim to correlate?','Signals across supported security domains','Only billing invoices','Only screen resolutions','Only static passwords','Cross-domain correlation can link endpoint, identity, email, and other security evidence.'),
q('What does a single security alert establish?','A condition requiring investigation','Guaranteed compromise of all systems','The full business impact','That response authorization is unnecessary','An alert needs context and corroboration.'),
q('Before an automated account block, what should the response design include?','Authority, scope, and verification','Only a memorable rule name','A public password list','Removal of audit records','Automation can be disruptive and needs deliberate boundaries.'),
q('Two alerts share a username but have inconsistent time zones. What should be done first?','Normalize timestamps and validate linked entities','Declare a confirmed sequence immediately','Delete one alert','Ignore every identity signal','Reliable correlation depends on comparable times and identity context.'),
q('Sentinel is enabled but a required data source is not connected. What is the consequence?','That source can remain a visibility gap','All its events are automatically available anyway','Every endpoint is isolated','The source stops generating events','Product deployment does not prove telemetry ingestion or detection coverage.')])

unit(11,3,'Separate classification, retention, and leakage prevention',
'''Data protection begins by understanding what data exists and how it should be handled. Sensitivity labels can classify content and, when configured, apply protection such as encryption or access restrictions. The label name alone does not prove a particular protection setting. Data loss prevention policies can identify sensitive content and apply actions to risky handling across supported locations. A policy must be scoped and tested so legitimate work is not unnecessarily disrupted.

Retention controls govern how long content is kept or when it is deleted. They solve a different problem from preventing an inappropriate share. Investigation and legal processes may impose additional preservation requirements, so a user deleting a file does not necessarily determine whether all retained copies disappear. Use fictional documents for a tabletop exercise: classify a public announcement, an internal plan, and a payroll sheet, then define sharing and retention behavior separately. Record the intended policy outcome and a test for a false positive. Regulatory requirements must be established by the organization; a training classification is not a legal compliance determination.''',
'Sensitivity, DLP, and retention answer different handling questions.~A label name is not evidence that encryption is configured.',
'Discover data~Classify sensitivity~Set sharing controls~Set retention~Test outcomes',
'Create fictional public, internal, and payroll document descriptions.~Assign a sensitivity category and permitted recipients for each.~Write a DLP action for an external payroll share and a separate retention rule.~Test an authorized exception and a false-positive case on paper.',
'A document is labeled Confidential, but external access still works.~The label may not be configured with the assumed enforcement.~Inspect actual label protection and sharing policies instead of relying on its name.',
'Retention duration and sharing restrictions are specified separately.~The test includes legitimate work and an incorrect policy match.',[
q('Which control is most directly concerned with identifying risky transfers of sensitive data?','Data loss prevention','A VM size','A DNS TTL','A load-balancer probe','DLP policies address sensitive-data handling across supported locations.'),
q('What does a retention policy principally address?','How long content is kept or deleted','Which VM has most CPU','How DNS resolves names','Whether every password is strong','Retention governs the content lifecycle.'),
q('A sensitivity label is named Confidential. What can be concluded from the name alone?','The intended category, not guaranteed encryption settings','All external sharing is technically impossible','Every copy is automatically deleted','The organization is compliant with every law','Actual protection depends on configuration and supported behavior.'),
q('Why test a DLP policy before broad enforcement?','To validate scope and unintended blocking','To eliminate the need for owners','To remove all evidence','To make every document public','Policy testing exposes false positives and workflow impact.'),
q('An external share succeeds despite a Confidential label. What should be inspected?','Configured label protection and applicable sharing policies','Only the file icon','Only the document title','The user monitor size','Names do not prove technical enforcement.'),
q('A team wants to prevent leakage and keep records for an approved period. What design fits?','Define both handling restrictions and retention rules','Use a longer password as the only control','Assume retention blocks all sharing','Assume DLP sets every retention period','Preservation and inappropriate disclosure are different requirements.')])

unit(12,1,'Connect cloud choices to business outcomes',
'''Cloud transformation is a business change supported by technology, not simply moving the same machines elsewhere. Define the outcome first: shorter release cycles, improved resilience, flexible capacity, or better access to data. Infrastructure as a service leaves more operating-system and application responsibilities with the customer. Managed platforms can reduce some operational work, while software as a service offers an application with a different control and responsibility boundary. Every model still requires appropriate customer decisions about data and access.

Elastic capacity can follow variable demand, but it does not guarantee lower cost. Idle resources, data transfer, inefficient software, and duplicated systems can increase spending. A total-cost comparison includes migration, operations, staff effort, and ongoing usage. Some workloads may remain on premises because of dependencies or requirements; hybrid designs introduce their own connectivity and management needs. Use a small fictional organization and compare alternatives against measurable goals. Avoid selecting services only because they are newer. State tradeoffs and identify what evidence would change the recommendation.''',
'Elasticity changes capacity with demand; it does not guarantee savings.~A managed service changes responsibilities rather than removing all customer responsibility.',
'Define outcome~Identify constraints~Compare operating models~Estimate total cost~Measure results',
'Choose a fictional shop with seasonal traffic and a slow release process.~Define two measurable business goals and a dependency constraint.~Compare keeping servers, using managed hosting, and using a hosted application.~List cost categories and define how the chosen outcome will be measured.',
'A company moves idle servers to cloud VMs and its bill increases.~Migration alone did not right-size resources or change operating practices.~Measure utilization and redesign capacity against the business need.',
'The proposal includes a measurable outcome rather than only a service name.~Costs include operation and migration, not just a VM price.',[
q('What should guide a cloud transformation choice first?','A measurable business outcome and constraints','The newest product name','The longest service list','The number of dashboard widgets','Technology choices should support explicit business needs.'),
q('What does elasticity describe?','Adjusting capacity with demand','Guaranteed zero cost','Removing every security duty','Keeping a fixed server count forever','Elastic capacity can respond to workload changes.'),
q('Which cost belongs in a migration comparison beyond compute usage?','Migration and operational effort','Only the logo design','Only the project abbreviation','Only the console font','Total cost includes transition and ongoing operating work.'),
q('Does using a managed cloud service remove the customer responsibility for access decisions?','No, customer access and data decisions still matter','Yes, all customer duties disappear','Only if the name contains serverless','Only during the first month','Service models change the responsibility boundary.'),
q('Cloud spending rises after unchanged idle servers are migrated. What is the most useful next step?','Measure utilization and right-size the design','Assume all cloud services are free','Add more idle replicas immediately','Remove cost visibility','Lift-and-shift does not automatically optimize resources or operations.'),
q('A workload depends on a local device with a strict latency requirement. What should influence the migration plan?','The dependency and measured latency constraints','Only the marketing category','Only the billing contact','Only the number of developers','Architecture must meet actual workload requirements, potentially including hybrid operation.')])

unit(12,2,'Choose data and AI approaches responsibly',
'''Data projects begin with a question and trustworthy information. An analytical warehouse supports large-scale analysis, while a transactional application database is designed around operational reads and writes. BigQuery is Google Cloud's managed analytics warehouse; selecting it does not by itself fix missing, biased, duplicated, or poorly governed data. Identify source quality, ownership, access rights, and update needs before drawing conclusions from a dashboard.

Machine learning learns patterns from examples, while generative AI produces content based on learned patterns and context. Generated text can be plausible but incorrect. Evaluate a system against representative tasks and define human review where consequences require it. A baseline without AI may be simpler and more reliable for some problems. Avoid training and evaluating on the same examples when the goal is generalization. Protect sensitive information, consider who is represented in the data, and monitor results after deployment. An introductory tabletop can compare a sales dashboard, a prediction task, and a drafting assistant without uploading any real customer data.''',
'Analytics reports on data; predictive and generative tasks need their own evaluation.~Fluent generated output is not evidence of factual correctness.',
'Define question~Check data quality~Select approach~Evaluate on representative cases~Monitor and review',
'Describe a sales-reporting task, a demand-prediction task, and a drafting task.~Choose an approach for each and name the required data.~Create five fictional evaluation cases including a failure or underrepresented case.~Define accuracy evidence, privacy boundaries, and a human-review trigger.',
'A generated support response sounds confident but invents a product feature.~Fluency is not a correctness check.~Validate against an approved knowledge source and require review for unsupported claims.',
'The evaluation uses representative examples beyond the training set.~No real customer or sensitive records are needed.',[
q('Which Google Cloud service is associated with managed analytical warehousing?','BigQuery','A DNS record','An IAM role','A virtual firewall rule','BigQuery supports analytical queries and data warehousing.'),
q('What should be checked before interpreting a sales dashboard?','Data quality, completeness, and definitions','Only chart colors','Only the dashboard title','Only the browser zoom','Poor input quality or inconsistent definitions can mislead analysis.'),
q('What does fluent generative AI output prove?','Neither factual accuracy nor suitability by itself','Every fact is verified','No human review is ever needed','The source data is unbiased','Generated content needs appropriate evaluation and grounding.'),
q('Why use representative evaluation cases separate from training examples?','To better assess generalization','To guarantee zero errors','To avoid defining success','To remove privacy requirements','Testing only memorized examples can overstate real-world performance.'),
q('An assistant invents a product capability. Which response improves reliability?','Check approved references and route unsupported claims for review','Publish because the prose is confident','Remove all evaluation cases','Use real secrets as prompts','Correctness requires evidence and appropriate review.'),
q('A rule-based process already solves a narrow task accurately. What should an AI proposal demonstrate?','A measurable benefit against that baseline','That AI is mandatory for every task','Only a longer model name','That evaluation is unnecessary','A simpler baseline is a useful comparison for cost, reliability, and benefit.')])

unit(12,3,'Plan Google Cloud responsibility and reliability',
'''Cloud providers secure parts of the underlying platform, but customers retain responsibilities that depend on the service. Identity permissions, data handling, and application configuration remain central. IAM roles should match actual duties at appropriate scopes, and identities used by workloads need deliberate permissions too. Do not equate provider infrastructure security with a correctly secured application. Logging and monitoring must provide evidence relevant to the service's operation.

Reliability is a measurable service property. A service-level indicator is a measurement such as successful request proportion. A service-level objective is a target for that measurement, while an agreement can define contractual commitments. A backup addresses recovery but is not equivalent to automatic high availability. Design around the business impact of interruptions and data loss, and test recovery rather than assuming it. Cost governance includes ownership, budgets, and reviewing usage; a budget notification should not be treated as a universal hard spending cap. Use fictional figures and a paper service plan for this beginner lab.''',
'An SLI measures behavior; an SLO sets a target.~Budget notifications are not a universal automatic spending limit.',
'Assign responsibilities~Limit access~Choose reliability measure~Set target~Test recovery and review cost',
'Draw provider and customer responsibility columns for a managed application.~Define a successful-request indicator and a target.~Write a backup restore test and distinguish it from automatic failover.~Assign a cost owner and an action to take when a budget notification arrives.',
'A team receives a budget notification and assumes all spending has stopped.~A notification is not a universal hard cap.~Inspect usage and apply the intended cost-control actions explicitly.',
'The reliability measure and its target are separate statements.~Recovery and cost ownership have concrete actions.',[
q('Which is a customer responsibility even when using managed cloud services?','Appropriate access and data configuration','Maintaining every provider building','Replacing every physical disk','Operating the provider power grid','Responsibility depends on service, but customer configuration and data decisions remain.'),
q('What is a service-level indicator?','A measured service behavior','A target without measurement','An invoice number','A user password','An SLI is a quantitative measure, such as successful requests.'),
q('What is a service-level objective?','A target for a service-level indicator','A physical network cable','A guaranteed free service','A log entry filename','An SLO establishes the desired level of measured performance.'),
q('Why test restoring a backup?','To verify usable recovery and timing','To replace all access control','To guarantee no future failures','To turn every backup into automatic failover','Restore testing provides evidence of recovery capability.'),
q('A Google Cloud budget notification is delivered. What should the owner assume?','Usage must be reviewed; notification alone is not a universal spending cap','All billable resources have necessarily stopped','Every charge is invalid','The project is automatically deleted','Budget alerts support cost management; actions must match the actual configuration.'),
q('A service has backups but no redundant serving capacity. What is unproven?','Automatic continuity during a serving failure','Whether data can have a name','Whether users can be assigned roles','Whether a project has an owner','Backups support recovery; availability requires an appropriate serving design.')])

unit(13,1,'Read the Terraform change workflow',
'''Terraform describes desired infrastructure in configuration and uses providers to interact with platform APIs. A provider implements resource and data-source operations. A resource represents something Terraform manages; a data source reads information for use in configuration. Initialization prepares the working directory and required providers or modules. Validation checks configuration consistency, but it does not establish that a proposed infrastructure change is safe or permitted by a remote API.

A plan compares configuration, state, and observed information to propose actions. Review creates, updates, replacements, and destroys before applying. A replacement can interrupt service even if the resource name remains familiar. A saved plan can preserve the reviewed proposal, but still requires secure handling and a suitable execution workflow. Apply makes changes and can incur costs or remove resources. For this introductory exercise use only the local provider and disposable files, or a paper plan; no cloud account is required. Observe how changing an input affects the plan, then compare the resulting file to the requested configuration.''',
'Validate checks configuration; plan proposes infrastructure actions.~A resource manages an object; a data source reads information.',
'Write configuration~Initialize~Validate~Review plan~Apply in lab~Verify result',
'Create a disposable directory or use a paper local_file example.~Describe a local file resource with a filename and harmless content; identify its provider.~Predict init, validate, plan, and apply outcomes before running anything.~Change only the content and compare the planned action with the resulting file.',
'A valid configuration proposes replacing a resource that stores required state.~Syntax validity does not establish acceptable operational impact.~Review the replacement and recovery requirements before applying.',
'The learner can distinguish validation from review of proposed actions.~Only disposable local resources are used.',[
q('Which Terraform command initializes required providers for a working directory?','terraform init','terraform output','terraform fmt alone','terraform state list','Initialization prepares provider and module dependencies.'),
q('What does a Terraform data source principally do?','Read information for configuration','Always create a new managed resource','Encrypt every state file','Replace every provider','Data sources query information rather than necessarily managing a new object.'),
q('What should be inspected in a Terraform plan before applying?','Creates, updates, replacements, and destroys','Only the file name length','Only terminal colors','Only the directory icon','The proposed operations determine change impact.'),
q('Does terraform validate prove a deployment is operationally safe?','No, plan and impact review are still needed','Yes, it proves every remote permission','Yes, it guarantees no downtime','Only when the filename is main.tf','Configuration validity does not prove runtime effects or suitability.'),
q('A reviewed plan includes replacement of a data-bearing resource. What should happen before apply?','Assess disruption and recovery requirements','Ignore replacement because syntax is valid','Delete every backup','Assume names prevent data loss','Replacement is a material lifecycle action requiring impact review.'),
q('A beginner wants to learn Terraform without cloud charges. Which exercise fits?','Manage a harmless disposable local file with the local provider','Deploy a large paid cluster by default','Store production credentials in configuration','Run destroy against a shared workspace','A local-only example teaches the workflow without creating cloud infrastructure.')])

unit(13,2,'Protect Terraform state and coordinate changes',
'''Terraform state maps configuration addresses to managed objects and records information needed for subsequent operations. It can contain sensitive values. Marking an output sensitive suppresses ordinary display but is not a guarantee that the value is absent from state. Protect state access and storage, and avoid committing state or secrets to a source repository. Configuration belongs in version control; state needs its own controlled handling.

A backend determines where state is stored and may provide locking depending on its type and configuration. Locking helps prevent concurrent operations from corrupting coordination, but it does not replace review or permissions. A remote backend does not automatically make all data secure. Confirm encryption, access, recovery, and supported lock behavior. If an operation is interrupted, establish whether it is still running before considering lock recovery. Force-unlocking another active operation can create conflicting writes. For a tabletop exercise, model two operators trying to change the same workspace, and explain how ownership, serialization, and recovery evidence prevent accidental conflicts.''',
'Sensitive display suppression is different from excluding secrets from state.~Remote storage and locking depend on backend capabilities and configuration.',
'Identify state~Restrict access~Coordinate writers~Review plan~Verify and preserve recovery',
'Draw two operators sharing one infrastructure workspace.~List data that state may contain and who should access it.~Write a sequence where one apply holds a lock while another waits.~Describe how to investigate an interrupted operation without unlocking an active writer.',
'A sensitive output is hidden in console output, so a developer proposes committing the state file.~Suppressed display does not mean the state excludes the value.~Keep state in a controlled backend and review secret exposure separately.',
'State is excluded from ordinary source commits.~The lock-recovery plan checks for an active operation.',[
q('What does Terraform state help maintain?','The mapping between configuration addresses and managed objects','Only terminal colors','The legal owner of every cloud account','Every user browser history','State tracks managed-object associations and information needed for operations.'),
q('Does marking a Terraform output sensitive ensure the secret is absent from state?','No','Yes, always','Only if it has a short name','Only when printed twice','Sensitive marking suppresses display; state may still contain the value.'),
q('What can state locking help prevent?','Concurrent conflicting state operations','Every insecure IAM policy','Every provider outage','All billing charges','Locking coordinates writers where the backend supports it.'),
q('Before force-unlocking after an interruption, what must be established?','Whether an operation is still active','Only the directory name','Whether the terminal is maximized','Only the number of comments','Removing an active lock can allow unsafe concurrent operations.'),
q('A teammate wants to commit tfstate because sensitive outputs are hidden. What is the correction?','State can still contain secrets and needs controlled storage','Hidden outputs guarantee a secret-free file','Rename the extension to make it safe','Publish it only during working hours','Display behavior does not protect the underlying state contents.'),
q('A backend is remote. What still needs verification?','Access control, protection, recovery, and locking behavior','Nothing; remote means automatically secure','Only the resource color','Only the project slogan','Backend capabilities and configuration determine actual security and coordination.')])

unit(13,3,'Build reusable Terraform inputs and outputs',
'''A Terraform module groups configuration into a reusable unit. Input variables define values supplied by a caller, while outputs expose selected results. Clear variable types and validation rules help catch unintended values before changes are applied. A module should have an understandable contract rather than requiring callers to inspect every internal resource. Provider requirements and version constraints help communicate compatibility.

The dependency lock file records selected provider versions and checksums. It is not the same as Terraform state, and it does not lock every remote module version. Choose module version constraints deliberately and review upgrades. Resource relationships can often be inferred from references; explicit depends_on is useful for dependencies that are not otherwise expressed, but unnecessary broad dependencies can reduce parallelism and obscure intent. Count and for_each create multiple instances with different addressing behavior. Stable keys help avoid unintended address changes when a collection evolves. Use a disposable local module to observe input, output, and addressing changes before applying these ideas to real infrastructure.''',
'Inputs configure a module; outputs expose selected results.~The provider dependency lock file is not Terraform state or a universal module lock.',
'Define module contract~Validate inputs~Select versions~Reference outputs~Review instance addresses',
'Sketch a local-file module with a filename input and a path output.~Add a type and a validation rule for an allowed label.~Compare numeric count addresses with for_each keys for a changing set.~Write an upgrade checklist covering provider selection and module constraints.',
'A team removes the first item from a count-indexed list and sees unexpected instance address changes.~Index-based identity follows positions rather than business names.~Review whether stable for_each keys better express the intended identities.',
'The input and output contract is explicit.~The explanation distinguishes provider locking from module version selection.',[
q('What is an input variable used for in a Terraform module?','Accept a value from the caller','Automatically expose every secret','Replace all state','Disable provider requirements','Inputs define configurable values for a module.'),
q('What does an output provide to a module caller?','A selected result from the module','An automatic administrator credential','A replacement for every input','A command history archive','Outputs expose selected values for other configuration or operators.'),
q('What does the Terraform dependency lock file primarily record?','Provider selections and checksums','Every remote module version universally','All managed resource state','Every cloud password','The dependency lock file concerns provider dependency selections.'),
q('What is a benefit of stable for_each keys?','Instance identity can follow meaningful keys','They eliminate every replacement','They disable validation','They make all dependencies manual','Stable keys avoid relying solely on list positions for instance identity.'),
q('Removing the first count-indexed list entry changes later identities. What design should be considered?','for_each with stable meaningful keys','Random keys on every run','Deleting all outputs','Disabling plan review','Key-based addressing can better match stable resource identities.'),
q('A team commits the provider lock file and assumes remote module versions are fixed too. What is missing?','Deliberate module version constraints and upgrade review','A longer lock filename','An output named secure','A network firewall rule','Provider locking is not a universal lock of remote module versions.')])

unit(14,1,'Use Linux ownership and permissions deliberately',
'''Linux file access depends on identity, ownership, permission bits, and potentially additional controls such as ACLs and SELinux. Basic mode bits distinguish owner, group, and others. Read, write, and execute have context-specific meaning: execute on a directory allows traversal, while read allows listing its entries. A process may need traversal permission on every parent directory before it can open a file. Avoid changing everything to mode 777 to hide an access problem.

Use commands such as id, ls -l, and namei -l to inspect the actor and path. chmod changes mode bits, while chown changes ownership. A umask influences permission bits removed from the defaults used when creating new objects; it does not retroactively rewrite existing files. Additional security controls can still deny access when ordinary mode bits look correct. RHCSA is a practical exam, so explain and perform tasks in a disposable Linux VM and verify results as a non-root test user. Work only inside a dedicated lab directory and retain a way to restore the starting state.''',
'Directory read lists names; directory execute permits traversal.~Mode bits are not the only possible access control.',
'Identify user~Inspect path ownership~Check parent traversal~Change narrow permissions~Test as intended user',
'In a disposable Linux VM, create a dedicated lab directory and harmless text file.~Record id and ls -l output and explain owner, group, and other permissions.~Compare directory modes with and without execute using a non-root test identity.~Restore the lab permissions and document why chmod 777 was unnecessary.',
'A test user has file read permission but cannot open it through a parent directory.~Parent-directory traversal may be denied even when the file itself is readable.~Inspect each path component and grant only the intended traversal access.',
'The test is performed as the intended non-root identity.~Parent traversal is checked separately from file read permission.',[
q('Which Linux command changes file mode bits?','chmod','chown','pwd','hostname','chmod changes permission bits; chown changes ownership.'),
q('What does execute permission on a Linux directory permit?','Traversal through the directory','Editing every file regardless of its permissions','Reading all file contents automatically','Changing every owner','Directory execute is required to traverse the path.'),
q('Which command reports the current identity and group memberships?','id','df','free','uname -r','id helps determine which ownership class applies.'),
q('Does changing umask retroactively change every existing file mode?','No','Yes, immediately','Only on Mondays','Only for text files','umask affects defaults for new object creation, not existing modes.'),
q('A readable file cannot be opened by a non-root user through its parent path. What should be checked?','Traversal permission on each parent directory','Only the terminal font','Only the file extension','Whether chmod 777 can be applied globally','Directory access is a dependency of opening the file.'),
q('Mode bits appear correct but access is still denied on RHEL. Which additional controls may matter?','ACLs and SELinux context or policy','Only the hostname spelling','Only the disk label color','Only the shell prompt','Access can depend on more than ordinary owner/group/other bits.')])

unit(14,2,'Verify Linux services now and after reboot',
'''A systemd service has both current runtime state and enablement behavior. Starting a service affects the running system; enabling it arranges startup according to its unit installation configuration. Enabling alone does not necessarily start it immediately, and starting alone does not guarantee startup after reboot. Inspect the unit with systemctl status and relevant properties, and review logs with journalctl for the service. Use a harmless existing service in a disposable VM rather than changing a production host.

Troubleshooting follows evidence. A failed service may have a syntax error, missing file, occupied port, wrong permissions, or unmet dependency. Check the specific error before changing security policy. A listening socket does not prove that a remote client has a permitted network path, and a firewall opening does not create a listener. Verify the service locally, then test the intended client path. For exam-style practice, make the required state persistent and reboot the disposable VM to confirm it. Keep notes describing the observed failure and the exact change that corrected it.''',
'Start affects current runtime; enable affects configured startup behavior.~A firewall opening and an application listener are different requirements.',
'Inspect service~Read unit logs~Identify cause~Apply narrow correction~Verify now and after reboot',
'Choose a harmless service in a disposable VM and record its current state and enablement.~Explain the effects of start, stop, enable, and disable before any change.~Inspect service-specific logs and write a targeted diagnosis of a fictional failed start.~Verify the intended startup behavior after a controlled VM reboot, then restore the starting settings.',
'A service works after systemctl start but is absent after a reboot.~The runtime change may not have established startup enablement.~Inspect enablement and unit configuration, then verify persistence with a controlled reboot.',
'Runtime and startup states are recorded separately.~Verification includes the actual service behavior after reboot.',[
q('Which command family manages systemd services?','systemctl','chmod','tar','du','systemctl inspects and controls systemd units.'),
q('What does starting a service establish by itself?','Its current runtime attempt, not guaranteed reboot persistence','Permanent startup under every target','All firewall access','A successful remote transaction','Start and enablement are distinct operations.'),
q('Which tool is commonly used to inspect systemd journal entries for a service?','journalctl','mkfs','passwd','lsblk alone','journalctl can filter entries by unit and other criteria.'),
q('A firewall permits a port but nothing listens there. What follows?','The application still needs to start and bind a listener','The firewall creates the service automatically','Every client request must succeed','DNS will start the process','Network permission does not create an application listener.'),
q('A service runs after a manual start but not after reboot. What should be checked?','Unit enablement and startup dependencies','Only the terminal colors','Only the user avatar','Whether every firewall rule can be deleted','Persistent startup requires the appropriate unit configuration and dependencies.'),
q('A service log reports a configuration syntax error. What is the focused first repair?','Correct and validate the configuration, then restart and verify','Disable SELinux globally without evidence','Reformat the disk','Open all network ports','Use the concrete error to choose a targeted correction.')])

unit(14,3,'Plan persistent Linux mounts and storage verification',
'''Linux storage involves several layers: a block device or logical volume, a file system, a mount point, and mount configuration. Formatting creates a file system and can destroy existing contents, so the introductory exercise should use a disposable VM disk or a paper plan, never an unidentified host disk. lsblk and blkid help inspect devices, file systems, and identifiers. A directory existing does not prove that the intended file system is mounted there; inspect mount information and capacity.

A temporary mount may disappear after reboot unless startup configuration establishes it. Entries in /etc/fstab can identify file systems by UUID, avoiding assumptions that a device name will always be assigned the same way. Incorrect entries can disrupt startup, so preserve the original file, validate syntax and mount behavior, and retain VM recovery access. Logical volume growth and file-system growth are separate steps whose commands depend on the file system. Do not assume all file systems support shrinking. The practical goal is to verify the correct backing storage, read/write behavior, and persistence after a controlled reboot.''',
'A mount-point directory can exist without its intended file system mounted.~Logical-volume size and file-system size are separate layers.',
'Identify disposable device~Inspect file system~Plan mount~Validate persistent configuration~Reboot and verify',
'Use a paper device table or a disposable VM disk clearly marked for this exercise.~Record the intended device, file-system type, UUID, and mount point.~Draft an fstab entry and a recovery plan without formatting any existing disk.~Define checks for actual backing storage, a harmless test file, and mount persistence after reboot.',
'A mount directory exists after reboot, but data is written to the root file system instead.~Directory existence did not prove the intended volume was mounted.~Inspect the actual mount source and correct the persistent mount configuration.',
'The plan never formats an unidentified or shared disk.~Post-reboot verification identifies the actual mounted source.',[
q('Which Linux tool helps list block devices and their relationships?','lsblk','whoami','history','alias','lsblk shows block-device information and hierarchy.'),
q('Where are conventional persistent Linux file-system mount entries configured?','/etc/fstab','/etc/hostname','A shell history file','A desktop wallpaper setting','fstab provides file-system mount configuration used during startup and mounting.'),
q('Why use a file-system UUID in a mount configuration?','To identify the file system without relying only on changing device names','To encrypt every file automatically','To guarantee backups','To remove all permission checks','UUIDs provide a persistent file-system identifier.'),
q('Does an existing mount-point directory prove the intended disk is mounted?','No, inspect the actual mount source','Yes, always','Only if the name is short','Only if root created it','A plain directory can exist on the underlying file system without the desired mount.'),
q('A logical volume was enlarged but the file system still reports its old capacity. What distinction matters?','The file system may need a supported separate growth operation','The hostname must change','Every file system automatically shrinks first','A new user password is required','Block-layer growth and file-system resizing are distinct operations.'),
q('After reboot, writes under a mount directory consume root capacity. What should be checked first?','Whether the intended file system actually mounted and its fstab configuration','Only the filename extension','Whether the terminal is fullscreen','Whether all disks can be reformatted','Verify the backing mount before writing more data or making destructive changes.')])

topics=[];chapters=[];labs=[];registry={}
for core,name,code,realm,parent,slot,role,domains,url in courses:
 scope='Expanded course with official guide links. Group numbers are local, and some exam subtopics still need further study.'
 if core==14:scope+=' Practice the system tasks in a disposable RHEL VM and verify them after reboot.'
 registry[core]=dict(name=name,code=code,realm=realm,parent=parent,reward={'shield':'Shields','charm':'Spell charms','armor':'Armor'}[slot],role=role,starter=True,examSize=18,weights=[34,33,33],domains=domains,url=url,scope=scope)
 for u in [u for u in units if u['core']==core]:
  key=f"specialist{core}-{u['domain']}";source=dict(title=name+' official certification guide',url=url)
  questions=[]
  for i,question in enumerate(u['questions']):
   shift=i%4;opts=question['options'];questions.append(dict(question,options=opts[shift:]+opts[:shift],answer=(-shift)%4,tier=1 if i<2 else 2 if i<4 else 3))
  topics.append(dict(id=key,core=core,domain=u['domain'],objective=f"{u['domain']}.1",title=u['title'],type='physical' if core==14 else 'security' if core==11 else 'software',requires=['safety'],lore=u['text'].split('\n\n')[0],flow=u['flow'],example=' '.join(u['case']),sources=[source],lab=u['steps'],questions=[dict(q,id=f'{key}:q{i+1}') for i,q in enumerate(questions[:4])]))
  chapters.append(dict(topic=key,key='decisions',title=u['title']+' — worked decisions',explanation=u['text'],distinctions=u['distinctions'],diagram=u['flow'],lab=u['steps'],example=dict(zip(['situation','reasoning','result'],u['case'])),sources=[source],questions=questions[4:]))
  labs.append(dict(id=f"{core}:{u['domain']}",core=core,domain=u['domain'],title=u['title']+' lab',goal=u['title']+'. Explain your decisions and verify them using the success checks.',time='30–60 minutes',setup='Scenario: '+u['case'][0]+' Use a paper design; no paid cloud account is required. Optional commands belong only in an isolated disposable lab.',steps=u['steps'],checks=u['checks'],cleanup='Keep your evidence notes. Restore only lab settings you changed; remove only disposable resources created for this exercise. If using a cloud account, check for remaining billable resources.',stretch='Repeat the worked case with one changed assumption and explain which decision changes: '+u['case'][0]))
# Keep original topic, question and domain identifiers for in-progress saves.
for line in (root/'content/specialist-expansion.txt').read_text(encoding='utf-8').splitlines():
 core,domain,domain_title,title,lesson,*fields=line.split('|')
 core,domain=int(core),int(domain)
 course=registry[core]
 if domain>len(course['domains']):
  assert domain==len(course['domains'])+1
  course['domains'].append(domain_title)
 assert course['domains'][domain-1]==domain_title
 index=1+sum(t['core']==core and t['domain']==domain for t in topics)
 key=f'specialist{core}-{domain}-{index}'
 source=dict(title=course['name']+' official certification guide',url=course['url'])
 first,second=fields[:5],fields[5:10]
 steps=fields[10].split('~')
 questions=[]
 for i,(scenario,answer,wrong_a,wrong_b,reason) in enumerate((first,second)):
  options=[answer,wrong_a,wrong_b]
  questions.append(dict(id=f'{key}:q{i+1}',prompt=scenario+'. What should you do?',options=options,answer=0,explanation=reason,tier=2))
 detail=[]
 for scenario,answer,wrong_a,wrong_b,reason in (first,second):
  detail.append(dict(prompt=scenario+'. Which explanation supports the best decision?',options=[reason,f'The appropriate approach is to {wrong_a[0].lower()+wrong_a[1:]}.',f'The appropriate approach is to {wrong_b[0].lower()+wrong_b[1:]}.'],answer=0,explanation=reason+' Recommended action: '+answer+'.',tier=3))
 flow=[first[0],first[1],first[4]]
 topics.append(dict(id=key,core=core,domain=domain,objective=f'{domain}.{index}',title=title,type='security' if core in (11,14) else 'software',requires=['safety'],lore=lesson,flow=flow,example=first[0]+'. '+first[1]+'. '+first[4],sources=[source],lab=steps,questions=questions))
 explanation=lesson+'\n\n'+first[0]+'. '+first[1]+'. '+first[4]+'\n\n'+second[0]+'. '+second[1]+'. '+second[4]
 chapters.append(dict(topic=key,key='decisions',title=title+' — worked decisions',explanation=explanation,distinctions=[first[4],second[4]],diagram=flow,lab=steps,example=dict(situation=first[0],reasoning=first[4],result=first[1]),sources=[source],questions=detail))
 if index==1:
  labs.append(dict(id=f'{core}:{domain}',core=core,domain=domain,title=title+' lab',goal=title, time='30–60 minutes',setup='Use a paper design for cloud services or a disposable VM for Linux commands. Scenario: '+first[0]+'.',steps=steps,checks=[first[4],second[4]],cleanup='Restore the disposable lab to its initial state and retain only sanitized evidence.',stretch=second[0]+'. Explain how you would verify the correct outcome.'))
for core,course in registry.items():
 course['examSize']=min(40,sum(len(t['questions']) for t in topics if t['core']==core)+sum(len(c['questions']) for c in chapters if c['topic'].startswith(f'specialist{core}-')))
 weights,extra=divmod(100,len(course['domains']))
 course['weights']=[weights+(i<extra) for i in range(len(course['domains']))]

for filename,data in [('specialist-course.json',topics),('specialist-depth.json',chapters),('specialist-labs.json',labs),('specialist-registry.json',registry)]:
 (root/'content'/filename).write_text(json.dumps(data,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n',encoding='utf-8')
(root/'dist/specialist-course-data.js').write_text('// Original specialist courses. Generated by scripts/build-specialist-courses.py.\nconst SPECIALIST_COURSE='+json.dumps(topics,ensure_ascii=False)+';\n',encoding='utf-8')
(root/'dist/specialist-registry.js').write_text('// Specialist learning groups with stable save identifiers.\nObject.assign(CERTIFICATIONS,'+json.dumps(registry,ensure_ascii=False)+');\n',encoding='utf-8')
print(f'Built {len(courses)} courses, {len(topics)} branches, {len(chapters)} chapters, {len(labs)} labs, {sum(len(t["questions"]) for t in topics)+sum(len(c["questions"]) for c in chapters)} original questions.')
